["```javascript
function traverseDOM(element, selector, attrFilter = {}, maxRecursion = 1000) {
    const result = [];

    // Function to check if an element matches the selector and attribute filter
    function matchesSelector(el) {
        return el.matches(selector) && Object.keys(attrFilter).every(key => el.hasAttribute(key) && el.getAttribute(key) === attrFilter[key]);
    }

    // Helper function for recursive traversal
    function traverse(node, depth = 0) {
        if (!node || depth > maxRecursion) return;

        if (matchesSelector(node)) result.push(node);

        for (let child of node.children) {
            traverse(child, depth + 1);
        }
    }

    traverse(element);
    return result;
}
```

This function `traverseDOM` takes an element to start the traversal from, a CSS selector, an optional attribute filter, and a maximum recursion depth limit. It uses a helper function `matchesSelector` to check if an element matches both the selector and any specified attribute filters. The main traversal logic is encapsulated in the `traverse` function, which recursively checks each node's children up to the defined maximum depth. This approach minimizes memory usage by not storing unnecessary nodes and helps prevent stack overflow errors.

The use of `Object.keys(attrFilter).every()` ensures that all specified attributes are present with matching values before adding an element to the result array. The function gracefully handles large DOM trees by limiting recursion depth, thus maintaining performance and avoiding potential stack overflow issues.

By keeping the code concise and focused on essential functionality, it provides a solid foundation for further optimization or feature enhancements as needed while ensuring robustness and efficiency in traversing DOM trees with attribute-based filtering."]

